I just built a "List Posts" endpoint in Spring Boot, and one small annotation taught me more than I expected: @Transactional(readOnly = true).
The endpoint filters published posts by category, tag, both, or neither:
@Override
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public List<Post> getAllPosts(UUID categoryId, UUID tagId) {
    if (categoryId != null && tagId != null) { ... }
    if (categoryId != null) { ... }
    if (tagId != null) { ... }
    return postRepository.findAllByStatus(PostStatus.PUBLISHED);
}
Why readOnly = true?
1. One transaction for several queries. Loading the category, then the tag, then the posts all share one persistence context.
2. Faster reads. Hibernate skips dirty checking and doesn't flush, because it knows nothing will be written.
3. Clear intent. Anyone reading the method knows it never modifies data.
4. Import matters. Use org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional. The Jakarta one has no readOnly attribute.
The other annotations doing the heavy lifting:
@RestController + @RequestMapping: expose the class as a REST API under /api/v1/posts
@GetMapping + @RequestParam(required = false): optional category and tag filters
@Service + @RequiredArgsConstructor: a service bean with constructor injection, no boilerplate
@Mapper(uses = {...}): MapStruct reuses my other mappers so postCount is calculated correctly
@Named: tells MapStruct which custom method to use for a field
Three lessons from this endpoint:
- Order your if statements. The "both filters" case must come first, or it gets swallowed.
- Hardcode the PUBLISHED status in the service. A public endpoint should never leak drafts.
- Return 404 for an unknown category ID instead of an empty list, because empty is not the same as wrong.
Still learning every day, and moving from React/TypeScript on the frontend toward Spring Boot on the backend.
What's the Spring annotation that surprised you the most?
